WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.580
Why does one hot region grow rainforest, another desert?

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.560
And where will warming push species?

3
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:13.300
Climate models turn 30-year averages of warmth and rain into a testable first guess

4
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.800
about where life occurs.

5
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.840
A Walter-Lieth diagram plots monthly temperature beside rain.

6
00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:29.280
A month reads dry when rain in millimetres falls below twice its temperature in °C.

7
00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:35.080
The factor two is an empirical plant-stress marker, not a water balance.

8
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:41.440
Köppen-Geiger rules sort climates in steps: aridity, then temperature class, then

9
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:42.440
subtype rules.

10
00:00:43.483 --> 00:00:47.363
Species distribution models link one species to climate.

11
00:00:49.063 --> 00:00:54.183
Following Hutchinson in 1957, climate variables become axes of niche space.

12
00:00:55.443 --> 00:01:00.343
A niche is the conditions where a population replaces itself without immigrants.

13
00:01:01.683 --> 00:01:04.763
The models fit the climates where the species was recorded.

14
00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:10.673
As climate warms, an isotherm, a line of equal temperature, climbs uphill.

15
00:01:11.953 --> 00:01:15.653
Its speed equals warming divided by cooling per 100 metres.

16
00:01:17.013 --> 00:01:24.293
Say a slope warms 0.3 °C a decade and cools 0.6 °C per 100 metres.

17
00:01:25.493 --> 00:01:27.033
How far uphill each decade?

18
00:01:32.583 --> 00:01:36.963
0.3 divided by 0.6 is 0.5.

19
00:01:38.063 --> 00:01:42.383
Times 100 metres, the isotherm climbs 50 metres each decade.

20
00:01:43.623 --> 00:01:47.303
The measured median uphill shift is 11 metres a decade.

21
00:01:48.783 --> 00:01:53.183
11 over 50 is 22%, so such a species falls behind.

22
00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:59.303
A range map blurs three things: tolerable conditions, neighbours, and places

23
00:01:59.303 --> 00:02:05.283
reached. Immigrants can sustain a sink population where local deaths beat births.

24
00:02:06.663 --> 00:02:08.783
Unreached suitable places look empty.

25
00:02:10.043 --> 00:02:15.523
So models map occupied conditions, not the fundamental niche, where a species could

26
00:02:15.523 --> 00:02:17.723
persist without competitors or enemies.

27
00:02:19.223 --> 00:02:22.283
Transplants inside, at and beyond the edge test this.

28
00:02:22.283 --> 00:02:27.483
If only beyond-range plantings fail, conditions there are implicated.

29
00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:31.007
Open weather stations miss forest shade.

30
00:02:32.347 --> 00:02:39.847
In 2019, De Frenne and colleagues found understories averaged about 4.1 °C cooler in

31
00:02:39.847 --> 00:02:40.887
maximum temperature.

32
00:02:42.187 --> 00:02:46.387
Fire, soils and forest loss also reshape vegetation and ranges.

33
00:02:47.827 --> 00:02:52.107
Good models report confidence and keep projections apart from observations.

34
00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:59.920
In 2018 and 2023, Hylke Beck and colleagues mapped Köppen-Geiger climates at about 1

35
00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.000
kilometre, with confidence levels.

36
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:11.360
About 5% % of non-Antarctic land changed major class between 1901 to 1930 and 1991

37
00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:19.640
to 2020. By 2071 to 2099, scenarios project 5% to 13%.

38
00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.760
A class change is not yet a biome change.

39
00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:28.303
In 2011, Chen, Thomas and colleagues pooled land range shifts.

40
00:03:29.843 --> 00:03:36.223
Median shifts were 16.9 kilometres poleward and 11 metres uphill per decade, largest

41
00:03:36.223 --> 00:03:37.563
where warming was greatest.

42
00:03:38.963 --> 00:03:46.023
In 2014, Hargreaves, Samis and Eckert reviewed 111 transplant tests beyond range

43
00:03:46.023 --> 00:03:51.003
edges. Only 46% percent placed the edge at a niche limit.

44
00:03:51.823 --> 00:03:54.543
Others pointed to dispersal limits or sinks.
